<!--
    kuna castobject (P6, sub-stage stack-frame-layout; option castobject on|off).

    Source: the 2026-09-27 address-of cast census.  The frame layout types a
    stack slot from its own loads and stores and, where two readings of the
    same bytes differ only in signedness, keeps the one the data-type ordering
    ranks more specific: uint4 over int4.  So an out-parameter the callee
    declares int4 * comes back uint4 and the call casts its address.

    gcc -O0 -fno-pie -fno-stack-protector -fcf-protection=none -ffreestanding
    -nostdlib, linked at 0x1000 (the bytes below are .text), with waitpid
    declared below and keep a global int *:

      waitpid(pid, st, o)  { *st = pid << 8; return pid + o; }
      exit_code(pid)       { int status;
                             if (waitpid(pid, &status, 0) < 0) return -1;
                             if ((status & 0x7f) == 0) return (status >> 8) & 0xff;
                             return -2; }
      kept(pid)            the same, after keep = &status;
      two_widths(pid)      the same, returning ((unsigned char *)&status)[1]
      mixed(pid, init)     int status = init; the same tests, then
                           return (unsigned)status >> 20;
      stored(pid, k)       unsigned x = (k >> 1) ^ 0x80000001u; status = x;
                           if (x & 0x10) return x >> 28;
                           waitpid(pid, &status, 0); return status >> 8;

    Pass 1 (option castobject off) reproduces the defect: exit_code's status is
    declared uint4, the call casts its address, and the arithmetic shift casts
    the value back (#1, #2, #3).  Pass 2 (option castobject on) declares it
    int4 as waitpid does, and both casts go (#4, #5, #6).  kept's address is
    also stored, and two_widths' status is also read one byte at a time; both
    keep their declaration in both passes (#7, #8).  mixed's status starts with
    a parameter's value and is also shifted right logically, and the value
    stored into stored's status is also shifted right logically on its own
    after the object's variable absorbs it: a reader wants the object unsigned,
    so both keep uint4 and the cast in both passes (#9, #10, #11).
-->
